# Every cross-document part whose framework differs from the one it is composed into. One row per
# crossing, with both frameworks and whether the composition cites an instrument.
#
# No schema change needed
#   Pair `regime` with `part` and the crossing is already there. `pm.regime` is a tall table
#   because a `filing.jurisdiction` column cannot hold `refutation`, which reports under two
#   regimes at once. `merge-group-composition` composes under IFRS-2026 from parts under `NCRF-PE`
#   and `us-gaap`, which is what consolidation is, and this is the relation that says so.
#
# The exact analogue of the unit crossing
#   `checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor` says: cross a unit boundary and you must file what
#   converts, because somebody is asserting two units are interchangeable and an assertion needs a
#   filer. Cross a regime boundary and somebody is asserting two frameworks' figures may be added.
#   `asrt:Composition/citation` carries the instrument and the clause, and that is what reconciles
#   it.
#
# No framework is not the same framework
#   A filing that states no framework is not a filing in the same framework, and SQL will say it
#   is. `IS DISTINCT FROM` returns false for two NULLs, so a pair of documents that never named a
#   regime reads as "no crossing" and vanishes from the population. `crossing_known` carries that
#   apart, and it is the §9 pattern arriving where nobody had looked for it: the matrix needs the
#   search beside it. Why a composition names no framework is typed in its own declaration,
#   `framework_absent` in composition/regimes.sqlc, and epistemics/absences.sqlc reports it; here
#   it only keeps the crossing unknown.
#
# `min` pairs the two halves because a term is whole
#   Where `states = 1`, `min` over the taxonomy and `min` over the value are taken separately and
#   still come from the one stated declaration, because the schema keeps every term whole: a
#   declaration filed absent has neither half. A taxonomy left beside an absence would pair with
#   another declaration's value.
#
# The composer's assignment, against the composition's own framework
#   The two frameworks compared are both the composer's: the one it assigns the part, filed as
#   `asrt:part/asrt:layer/asrt:regime` and read through composition/part_regimes.sqlc, and the one
#   the composition itself reports under. That is the right pair for a crossing, because the
#   composer is the one asserting the two frameworks' figures may be added. Whether the part's
#   own filing agrees with the composer's assignment is a different claim with a different author,
#   and `checks/part_regime_disagrees` asks it over the same relation: what a filing says about
#   itself and what somebody says about it are never the same claim.
#
# Why not `seq = 1`
#   `seq = 1` would be a guess that could not announce itself. A filing may declare more than one
#   regime, which is the whole reason `regime` is tall, and taking the first silently picks one of
#   them. `states` counts them, and a composition reporting under two frameworks makes "the
#   framework it composes into" a question with two answers, so the crossing is not known rather
#   than absent.
#
# Probe
#   Give the composition a second regime declaration: `crossing_known` must turn false for its
#   parts. A crossing count that does not move is the danger, not the reassurance.
-- composition/part_regimes.sqlc: the composer's framework for each part against the framework
-- the composition itself reports under.
SELECT x.composition, x.composed_layer, x.part_filing, x.part_layer,
       own.framework_taxonomy AS composed_taxonomy,
       own.framework_value    AS composed_framework,
       x.composer_taxonomy    AS part_taxonomy,
       x.composer_value       AS part_framework,
       (own.states = 1 AND x.composer_value IS NOT NULL)                  AS crossing_known,
       (own.states = 1 AND x.composer_value IS NOT NULL
        AND (own.framework_taxonomy, own.framework_value)
            IS DISTINCT FROM (x.composer_taxonomy, x.composer_value))     AS crosses,
       c.instrument, c.clause
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/part_regimes.sqlc)
) x
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT r.filing,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE r.framework_value IS NOT NULL) AS states,
           min(r.framework_taxonomy) AS framework_taxonomy,
           min(r.framework_value)    AS framework_value
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/regimes.sqlc)
    ) r
    GROUP BY r.filing
) own ON own.filing = x.composition
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/citations.sqlc)
) c ON c.composition = x.composition
